Write microagent.py: a single-file, self-contained agentic coding assistant that works on an existing code base through tool calls. Python 3.10+, dependencies only `openai` and `httpx`. Output ONLY the raw contents of the file, starting with `#!/usr/bin/env python3`. No markdown fences, no explanation before or after.

Configuration from environment variables: GATEWAY_URL (OpenAI-compatible base URL of a LiteLLM gateway), GATEWAY_KEY, MODEL, optional CA_BUNDLE (path to a CA file, used as httpx verify=), optional TLS_VERIFY=false (verify=False, print a one-line warning). Build the client as OpenAI(base_url=..., api_key=..., http_client=httpx.Client(verify=..., timeout=120)).

Command line: `python microagent.py --repo PATH "task"` runs one task and exits; `python microagent.py --repo PATH` with no task starts a REPL (`task> ` prompt, `exit`/`quit` to leave, conversation history kept across tasks); `--verify` makes one cheap call ("reply with the single word: ok"), prints the result and exits; `--max-turns N` (default 80).

Tools, all sandboxed to the --repo directory (resolve every path and refuse anything outside it):
- read_file(path, start_line=1, end_line=-1): line-numbered output "N<TAB>line", optional range.
- write_file(path, content): create or overwrite, making parent dirs.
- append_file(path, content).
- str_replace(path, old_str, new_str): old_str must match exactly once; if 0 or more than 1 matches, return an error message telling the model to re-read and send a longer unique snippet. Never raise.
- list_files(path=".", pattern="*"): recursive, fnmatch on file names, skip .git, node_modules, __pycache__, .venv, venv, .mypy_cache, .pytest_cache, dist, build, .idea; cap at 500 entries with a "(truncated)" line.
- grep(pattern, path=".", glob="*"): Python regex over every matching file, output "relpath:lineno: line", cap at 300 hits, skip binary/undecodable files silently.
- bash(command): subprocess with shell=True, cwd=repo, 300 s timeout, return "[exit N]" plus stdout and stderr; on timeout return an error string.
Expose each tool to the model as an OpenAI tool spec with a JSON schema; keep descriptions short and concrete.

Agent loop: messages = [system, user task]; call client.chat.completions.create(model, messages, tools, temperature=0.2, max_tokens=8192); append the assistant message as msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True) and, if it has tool_calls but no "content" key, set "content": "" (some gateway-to-Anthropic paths reject the turn otherwise); if there are no tool_calls, print msg.content and stop; else for each tool call parse the JSON arguments (an unparsable argument string becomes an error result, not a crash), print "  -> name(args truncated to 160 chars)", run the tool, catch every exception into "ERROR: <type>: <message>", truncate any result longer than 40,000 chars with a "(truncated)" marker, and append {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": id, "content": result}. After max turns print "[stopped: hit max turns, say 'continue' to keep going]".

System prompt: an expert coding agent working on the local repository at <absolute path>; explore with list_files and grep before reading, read before editing, prefer str_replace over rewriting files, after any change run the relevant tests or at least `python -m py_compile` via bash, never git push, and finish with a plain-text summary of what changed and how it was verified. If <repo>/AGENT.md exists append it under "# Project instructions"; append each <repo>/skills/*.md under "# Skill: <name>".

Code quality: type hints, a short module docstring that shows the setup and run commands, no global state other than the client and config, no dead code. It must run as-is.
